 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to allowing two\nautomatic free transfers for riders using pay-per-ride Metrocards",
    "basePrintNo": "S2779",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2779-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1266, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2779\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to allowing two\nautomatic free transfers for riders using pay-per-ride Metrocards\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo ensure that riders of the Metropolitan Transportation Authority mass-\ntransit system do not incur extra costs due to recent cuts in service.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of this bill amends section 1266 of the Public Authorities\nLaw by adding a new subdivision (3-b) that requires the Metropolitan\nTransportation Authority (MTA) to automatically apply two free transfers\nto each paid fare or rate and be valid for two hours from the time the\nfare is purchased. These transfers can used to transfer between subways\nand the buses that are owned and operated by the MTA as well as trans-\nfers between the MTA's buses. This transfer policy may be amended from\ntime to time.\n\nSection two of this bill provides that this act shall take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nCURRENT LAW:\n\nCurrently, holders of unlimited ride MetroCards have unlimited trans-\nfers. Holders of pay-per-ride MetroCards receive only 1 free transfer.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, upon purchasing a pay-per-ride MetroCard, the rider is\nallowed one automatic free transfer per fare. Unfortunately, due to\nrecent cutbacks in services and the outright elimination of various bus\nand subway lines, some riders now must purchase two fares to make their\ndaily commute. Essentially the MTA has imposed new fares on riders who\nhave enough trouble paying for one fare. The avoidable burden caused by\nthese cutbacks, which is the additional financial burden, should not be\npassed on to riders in these affected areas.  This bill would ensure\nthat no commuter or rider financially suffers due to cutbacks in service\nthat already add time and stress to a commute.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018: S.7862, Vetoed.\n2017: S.2560 - Vetoed, Memo259\n2016: S.6484 - Referred to Transportation\n2015: S.3228 - Vetoed, Memo 254\n2014: S.2092-A - Passed the Senate\n2013: S.2092 - Referred to Transportation\n2011-12: S.3839 - Referred to Transportation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
